# Preview ≠ Live — the permissive surface is the one you develop against

## The rule

Preview and live Edge are **two runtimes with two contracts**, and preview is the **permissive**
one. It is also the only surface the pipeline ever looks at: local dev, `/test`, `/test-content`
and the `/ship` deploy are **all** on the lenient side by design (rule `71`, preview-first). Any
contract the two contexts do not share is therefore **structurally invisible until `/publish`** —
not under-tested, *unobservable*.

Two consequences, both load-bearing:

1. **A check that passes against preview proves nothing about live** for anything in a divergence
   class. Green gates are not evidence here; they are evidence about the lenient runtime.
2. **Where a contract can differ, get ONE assertion against the strict surface** — even if it is
   environment-bound, manual, and run once by hand. One live observation beats any number of
   preview-side passes.

## Known divergence classes — all measured, list is OPEN

| Class | Preview (permissive) | Live (strict) | The trap |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Route-path form** | resolves the slugified path **and** the percent-encoded one | `site.routes` index carries **only the slugified** path (`/stories/Istanbul-In-Winter`) | `encodeURIComponent(itemName)` in a path segment works in *all* development and 404s for real visitors |
| **Media resolution** | media renders **without a publish** (resolves from the CM) | media requires the publish | "it renders" during authoring says nothing about go-live |
| **Cache / tag semantics** | single-process local `next start`: ISR windows and `revalidateTag` are immediate and total | multi-instance deployment: per-instance caches, alias + CDN layers | a revalidation proven locally is not proven in production |
| **PPR resume** (Cache Components) | local `next start` renders each page in one pass | Vercel serves the cached shell, then a separate resume invocation fills the postponed holes | an empty resume serves every page cut at the shell, HTTP 200, while every local gate is green (live 2026-10-15). Strict-surface assertion: `check-served-html.mjs` on a Vercel Preview (content-sdk pack test axis `served-html-preview-proof`) |

**The list is open — a divergence is *any* contract the two contexts do not share.** Routing,
media, cache, auth scoping, item resolution, host/header handling. When you meet a plausible
difference that is not listed, treat it as a divergence until one live assertion says otherwise
(rule `70` — that is a probe, not a build task), and add the class here once measured.

⚠️ Skills that state "media renders only AFTER publish" flatly (`sitecoreai-graphql-schemas`
and the imagery recipe) are describing the **live** context only. Read them as live-scoped, not
universal.

## The evidence (a prior run, 2026-08-11)

Four links were dead for real visitors the moment the site went live — `/destinations/Hong%20Kong`,
`/stories/Istanbul-In-Winter` and two siblings — while `/destinations/Hong-Kong` returned 200.
Seven call sites had each interpolated `encodeURIComponent(itemName)` into a path. Nothing caught
it: 658 passing tests, four review cycles, a full `/test` pass and a `/ship` deploy, all against
preview, which resolves both forms.

The sharpest part: `scripts/smoke-prod-routes.mjs` — a *production-build* route smoke written
precisely to catch what dev hides — **asserted `/destinations/Hong%20Kong` and passed for days**.
It ran `next start` against the preview context, so it did not merely miss the defect; it **pinned
the broken URL form as the expected value**. A check on the lenient side can encode the bug.

## What this changes in practice

- **Name the environment in every route/URL assertion** — see `30-tdd.mdc` § "Environment-scoped
  assertions". A test's verdict is scoped to the runtime it ran in.
- **Never treat a preview-side pass as go-live coverage** for a divergence class. Say what it
  proves (`no route 500s under a real production build`) and what it does not (`live-Edge routing`).
- **Budget the live assertion up front.** It belongs in the task breakdown's § 8 as an explicit,
  environment-bound item — not as a hope that `/publish` will notice.
- **`/publish` is the first strict-side observation of the whole run.** Its go-live check is not a
  formality; for these classes it is the only real evidence the run ever produces. Record what it
  showed.

## Agent application

- **04 Software Architect:** when a design emits URLs from content-derived names, depends on media
  being resolvable, or leans on cache/tag invalidation, name the divergence in the ADR's
  consequences and state which surface will prove it.
- **06 Lead Developer:** every route/URL/media/cache assertion in § 8 names its context. Any
  contract that differs across contexts gets **one** live-side item, marked environment-bound and
  manual if that is what it takes.
- **07 QA Specialist:** reject an assertion that does not name its environment. A preview-side URL
  check is not coverage for live — call that out in the test report rather than counting it green.
- **08 Developer:** if you are about to build a URL path segment, resolve a media URL, or rely on
  revalidation, ask which context you have actually seen it work in. `encodeURIComponent` belongs
  in **query values**, not path segments built from Sitecore item names.
- **13 DevOps Engineer:** at `/publish`, verify the divergence-class items on the live surface
  (routes 200, media 200-not-302) and report them as the run's first strict-side evidence.
- **00 Team Lead:** a run whose only route/media evidence is preview-side has an **open** gap at the
  `go_live` gate — surface it, do not let review-clean + test-green read as go-live-safe.

